steps.body:
Most pages in a shop depend on who's looking: the cart, the account page, the review form. Each one asks the same question, who is this, and each must answer it the same way, quickly, and without forgetting. Next.js's answer is a session cookie read on the server, and a **data access layer**: one server-only module that every read and write of private data goes through.

## The session cookie

The Trailhead API keeps sessions. Log in, and it answers with a `th_session` cookie holding a random session ID. The browser never talks to the API itself, though. It talks to your Next.js server, so the shop's login action, a Server Action on the login page, takes the ID from the API's answer and sets the same cookie on your own site:

```ts sketch
const token = /th_session=([^;]+)/.exec(res.headers.get("set-cookie") ?? "")?.[1];
if (!token) throw new Error("The API sent no session cookie");

(await cookies()).set("th_session", token, {
  httpOnly: true,
  secure: true,
  sameSite: "lax",
  path: "/",
  maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 7,
});
```

These are the flags from the lesson on Route Handlers and proxy, and `httpOnly` matters most here: it hides the cookie from JavaScript in the page, so an XSS bug can't read it. As that lesson explained, a cookie can be set only in a Server Action or a Route Handler, never while a page renders. The third example shows what the browser stored.

From then on, the browser sends `th_session` with every request to your site, and your server forwards it to the API when it fetches that visitor's data. That's the `session: true` option of `apiFetch`, from the lesson on fetching data: it reads the cookie and adds `Cookie: th_session=...` to the API request.

## verifySession(), asked once per render

Checking a session means asking the API who owns it, with `GET /me`. Until now, the account page did that with `getCurrentUser` from `lib/session.ts`, in the lesson on fetching data. The data access layer takes over that job. One page might need the answer in five components, and five requests would be slow. React's `cache()`, from the data lessons, fixes that: it remembers a function's result for the rest of one render, so the first call asks and the others reuse the answer.

`lib/dal.ts`, the first example, builds two functions on it:

- `getSession()` returns `{ userId, name }`, or `null` when there's no cookie or the API answers 401.
- `verifySession()` calls it and redirects to `/login` on `null`, so code after it can count on a session.

On the account page, `Greeting` calls `verifySession()`, `Cart` calls `getCartSummary()`, which calls `verifySession()` too, and the site header's cart link asks for the session as well. Reloading the page under `next dev`, with the fetch logging from the caching lesson, showed one `GET /v1/me` for all three, and two `GET /v1/cart`, one for the header's count and one for the account's, since `getCartSummary()` itself isn't cached. In a Server Action or a Route Handler, `cache()` does nothing, since those aren't part of a render, so each call there asks again.

Reading `cookies()` is request data, so with Cache Components the components that call `verifySession()` sit inside a Suspense boundary, and the rest of the page is prerendered around them, as the second example shows. The `"use cache: private"` variant from the caching lesson can keep a session-based result in the browser between navigations.

## The data access layer returns DTOs

A **data access layer**, or DAL, is the one place your app reads and writes private data. Every function in it checks the session first, then fetches, then returns a **DTO**, a data transfer object: a small plain object with only the fields the page needs. `getCartSummary()` returns `{ count, subtotalCents }`, not the API's whole cart. Server Components pass that shape on to Client Components as props, so a DTO decides what can reach the browser. Keep `lib/dal.ts` server-only, and have pages, actions, and Route Handlers call it instead of calling the API themselves. Then every access rule lives in one file a reviewer can read.

## Logging out

Logging out has to end the session on the server too, not just in the browser, or a copied cookie keeps working. The logout action asks the API to end the session, deletes the cookie, and goes home:

```ts sketch
export async function logout() {
  const store = await cookies();
  const token = store.get("th_session")?.value;
  if (token) {
    await fetch(`${env.API_URL}/auth/logout`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { Cookie: `th_session=${token}` },
    });
  }
  store.delete("th_session");
  redirect("/");
}
```

## Libraries

Sessions involve a lot to get right: hashing passwords, signing in with Google or GitHub, expiry, two-factor codes. **Auth.js**, which used to be called NextAuth.js, and **Better Auth** are popular open-source libraries that do it inside your Next.js app, and hosted services like Clerk and Auth0 do it for you. Whichever you pick, keep this shape: one function that reads the session, called from your DAL.

examples.code:
import "server-only";
import { cache } from "react";
import { cookies } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";
import { apiFetch } from "./api";
import { env } from "./env";
import { z } from "zod";

export type Session = { userId: string; name: string };

export const getSession = cache(async (): Promise<Session | null> => {
  const token = (await cookies()).get("th_session")?.value;
  if (!token) return null;

  const res = await fetch(`${env.API_URL}/me`, {
    headers: { Cookie: `th_session=${token}` },
  });
  if (res.status === 401) return null;
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`GET /me answered ${res.status}`);

  const me: { id: string; name: string } = await res.json();
  return { userId: me.id, name: me.name };
});

export async function verifySession(): Promise<Session> {
  const session = await getSession();
  if (!session) redirect("/login");
  return session;
}

export type CartSummary = { count: number; subtotalCents: number };

const CartSchema = z.object({
  items: z.array(z.object({ quantity: z.number().int() })),
  subtotalCents: z.number().int(),
});

export async function getCartSummary(): Promise<CartSummary> {
  await verifySession();
  const cart = CartSchema.parse(await apiFetch("/cart", { session: true }));
  return {
    count: cart.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.quantity, 0),
    subtotalCents: cart.subtotalCents,
  };
}

import { Suspense } from "react";
import { getCartSummary, verifySession } from "@/lib/dal";
import { logout } from "./actions";

async function Greeting() {
  const session = await verifySession();
  return <p>Hi, {session.name}.</p>;
}

async function Cart() {
  const cart = await getCartSummary();
  return <p>{cart.count} items in your cart, ${(cart.subtotalCents / 100).toFixed(2)}.</p>;
}

export default function AccountPage() {
  return (
    <section>
      <h1>Your account</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading your account...</p>}>
        <Greeting />
        <Cart />
      </Suspense>
      <form action={logout}>
        <button>Log out</button>
      </form>
    </section>
  );
}

import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from "next/server";

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  if (!request.cookies.has("th_session")) {
    const login = new URL("/login", request.url);
    login.searchParams.set("next", request.nextUrl.pathname);
    return NextResponse.redirect(login);
  }
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/account/:path*"],
};

import { getCartSummary, getSession } from "@/lib/dal";

export async function GET() {
  const session = await getSession();
  if (!session) {
    return Response.json({ title: "Unauthorized", status: 401 }, { status: 401 });
  }
  return Response.json(await getCartSummary());
}

import type { NextConfig } from "next";

const csp = [
  "default-src 'self'",
  "script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'",
  "style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'",
  "img-src 'self' blob: data:",
  "font-src 'self'",
  "connect-src 'self'",
  "object-src 'none'",
  "base-uri 'self'",
  "form-action 'self'",
  "frame-ancestors 'none'",
].join("; ");

const securityHeaders = [
  { key: "Content-Security-Policy", value: csp },
  { key: "Strict-Transport-Security", value: "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains" },
  { key: "X-Content-Type-Options", value: "nosniff" },
  { key: "Referrer-Policy", value: "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" },
  { key: "Permissions-Policy", value: "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()" },
];

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  cacheComponents: true,
  poweredByHeader: false,
  async headers() {
    return [{ source: "/:path*", headers: securityHeaders }];
  },
};

export default nextConfig;

import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from "next/server";

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const nonce = Buffer.from(crypto.randomUUID()).toString("base64");
  const csp = [
    "default-src 'self'",
    `script-src 'self' 'nonce-${nonce}' 'strict-dynamic'`,
    `style-src 'self' 'nonce-${nonce}'`,
    "img-src 'self' blob: data:",
    "font-src 'self'",
    "object-src 'none'",
    "base-uri 'self'",
    "form-action 'self'",
    "frame-ancestors 'none'",
  ].join("; ");

  const requestHeaders = new Headers(request.headers);
  requestHeaders.set("x-nonce", nonce);
  requestHeaders.set("Content-Security-Policy", csp);

  const response = NextResponse.next({ request: { headers: requestHeaders } });
  response.headers.set("Content-Security-Policy", csp);
  return response;
}

export const config = {
  matcher: [
    {
      source: "/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)",
      missing: [
        { type: "header", key: "next-router-prefetch" },
        { type: "header", key: "purpose", value: "prefetch" },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

